#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130459 :tutorial/content "Consider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ of mass $M$ where the center of mass is at $\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right).$\n\n![](images/4c/4c2c7e3525dfe8aea3cce69a7332ccf5ca82ddfe37657071ddef35650bcea383.png)\n\nWe know that the lamina has some moment of inertia $I_M$ about the line $l_M$ that passes through the center of mass and is perpendicular to the lamina.\n\n![](images/96/9638e9d47ef3c9b2fab47f39cf70e2ba07863c6e949755c8378e5737caa0a350.png)\n\nNow, if $l$ is a line parallel to $l_M,$ then the lamina also has moment of inertia about $l,$ say $I_l.$\n\n![](images/06/06819ca45bd176ff9d36b07c73574bc9f2911a7b0b96b5b916ba76f8606d2f60.png)\n\nGiven the value of $I_M,$ we can find $I_l$ using the **parallel axis theorem**, which states that\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose the lamina above has mass [math]4\\,\\rm{kg}[/math] and the moment of inertia about the line [math]l_M[/math] perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is [math]6.4\\rm{kg}\\cdot\\rm{m}^2.[/math]\n\nIf the other line [math]l[/math] parallel to [math]l_M[/math] is at a distance of [math]d=2\\,\\rm{m}[/math] from it, then, by the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about [math]l[/math] is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nI_l\t&= I_M + d^2M \\\\[3pt]\n\t&= 6.4 + 2^2 \\cdot 4 \\\\[3pt]\n\t&= 6.4 + 16 \\\\[3pt]\n\t&= 22.4 \\,\\rm{kg} \\cdot \\rm{m}^2.\n\\end{align*}\n\nThe term $d^2M$ is the moment of inertia about $l$ of the lamina when its entire mass is concentrated at the center of mass.\n\n![](images/fb/fbab9fab43dd45fd80008704f406f94864cac57d636320d250dca86ff465f196.png)\n\nSo, our interpretation of the parallel axis theorem is the following:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about a parallel line }l}_{I_l}\n\\:=\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about the center of mass}}_{I_M}\n\\:+\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of point mass }M \\ \\text{about the parallel line } l}_{d^2M}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d^2M$ is always positive, we have $I_M \\leq I_l$ for any line $l$ perpendicular to the lamina. In other words, the moment of inertia about the line through its center of mass is the *smallest* moment of inertia about any line in that direction." :tutorial/id #uuid "b1dda94b-0ea5-5ff2-a3d5-a406dfc9b95d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7332 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130480 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw that the moment of inertia about an axis $l$ of a plane lamina occupying the region $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D [r(x,y)]^2\\,\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of $D$ about the line through the center of mass $\\left(\\bar{x}, \\bar{y}\\right)$ and perpendicular to the lamina is given by\n\n$$\nI_M = \\iint\\limits_D \\left((x-\\bar{x})^2+(y-\\bar{y})^2\\right) \\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6a08f490-ccca-5a93-adb8-dbf845e4d4cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7849 :tutorial/title "A Formula for the Moment of Inertia About the Line Through the Center of Mass"}]}
